== Chapter X

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[An exhortation to love God.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

#emph[Use] 3. The third use is one of exhortation,#footnote[#emph[Use] 1. Exhortation.] and it has three branches. 1. Let me earnestly persuade all who bear the name of Christians to become lovers of God (Ps 31:23):#footnote[1\. #emph[Branch]] “Love the #smallcaps[Lord], all His saints.” There are only a few who love God. Many give him a hypocritical kiss, but few love him. It is not as easy to love God as most imagine. The #emph[affection] of love is natural, but the #emph[grace] is not. By nature men are θεοστυγεῖς, #emph[God-haters] (Rom 1:30). The wicked would flee from God; they would be neither under his rules nor within his reach. They #emph[fear] God, but they do not love him. All the strength in men or angels cannot make the heart love God. Ordinances will not do it of themselves, nor will judgments. Only the almighty and invincible power of the Spirit of God can pour love into the soul. Since this is so hard a work, it calls on us all the more to pray and labor earnestly for this angelic grace of love#footnote[#emph[Impium est Deum non diligere, cui vicem rependere non possumus, etiamsi diligamus.] \[It is wicked not to love God, whom we cannot repay even if we love him.\] Augustine.]. To stir up and kindle our desires for it, I will set down twenty motives.

=== Motives to Love God

1\. Without this, all our religion is vain. It is not duty, but love to duty, that God looks at; it is not how much we do,#footnote[1\. #emph[Motive.]] but how much we love. If a servant does not do his work willingly and out of love, it is not accepted. Duties not mixed with love are as burdensome to God as they are to us. That is why #emph[David] counsels his son #emph[Solomon] to serve God with a willing mind (1 Chr 28:9). To do duty without love is not #emph[sacrifice] but #emph[penance.]

2\. Love is the most noble and excellent grace. It is a pure flame kindled from heaven. By it we resemble God, who is #emph[love.] Believing and obeying do not make us like God, but by love we grow like him (1 John 4:16). Love is the grace that most delights in God, and it is most delightful to him. The disciple who was most full of love lay on Christ’s bosom. Love puts freshness and luster on all the graces; the graces seem to be eclipsed unless love shines and sparkles in them. Faith is not true unless it is “working through love.” The waters of repentance are not pure unless they flow from the spring of love. Love is the #emph[savory food] God loves. It is the incense that makes all our services sweet and fragrant. It is #emph[vinum aromaticum,] “the spiced wine” and “the nectar of the pomegranate.”

3\. Is anything God requires unreasonable? It is only our love. If he asked for our property, or for the fruit of our bodies, could we deny him? But he asks only for our love. He would only pick this flower. Is this a hard request? Was there ever a debt so easily paid as this? We do not make ourselves poorer at all by paying it. Love is no burden. Is it any labor for the bride to love her husband? Love is delightful: #emph[Non potest amor esse, et dulcis non esse]#footnote[#emph[Bernard.]] \[Love cannot be, and not be sweet\].

4\. God is the most fully suited and complete object of our love. All the excellencies that lie scattered among the creatures are twisted together and united in him. He is a storehouse of blessings. He is wisdom, beauty, love; indeed, the very essence of goodness. He is #emph[Optimus Maximus] \[the best and greatest\]. There is nothing in God that can cause disgust or loathing. The creature sooner surfeits than satisfies, but fresh beauties keep sparkling forth in God; the more we enjoy of him, the more we are ravished with delight.

There is nothing in God to dull our affections or quench our love: no weakness, no deformity, such as usually weaken and cool love. There is such a delicious sweetness in God that it may not only #emph[entice] but #emph[command] our love. Suppose there were more angels in heaven than there are, and all those glorious seraphim had an immense flame of love burning in their breasts to eternity. Still they could not love God in a measure equal to the infinite perfection and surpassing goodness that is in him. Surely, then, here is enough to draw us to fall in love with God. We cannot spend our love on a better object.

5\. Love makes religion easy. It oils the wheels of the affections and makes them more lively and cheerful in God’s service. Love takes away the tediousness of duty. #emph[Jacob] thought seven years only a little, for the love he had for #emph[Rachel.] Love makes duty a pleasure. Why are the angels so swift and winged in God’s service? It is because they love him. Love is never weary. He who loves gold is never weary of counting it, and he who loves God is never weary of serving him.

6\. God desires our love. It would be a great thing for a king to desire the love of a woman who was deformed and leprous. We have lost our beauty and stained our blood, yet the King of heaven is a suitor to us. What is there in our love that God should come wooing for it? How is God any better for our love? He does not need it; he is infinitely blessed in himself. If we deny him our love, he has higher creatures who pay him the cheerful tribute of love. God does not need our love, yet he seeks it.

7\. God has deserved our love. How he has loved us! Our affections should be kindled at the fire of God’s love. What a miracle of love it is that God should love us when there was nothing lovely in us! Ezek 16:6: “As you lay there in your blood I said to you, ‘Live!’” The time of our loathsomeness was the time of God’s love. We had something in us to provoke fury, but nothing to attract love. What a surpassing love it was to give Christ to us#footnote[#emph[Si amor augeatur in fonte, necesse est & in fluxu.] \[If love increases at the spring, it must increase in the stream too.\] William of Paris.]! That Christ should die for sinners: God has set all the angels in heaven wondering at this love. #emph[Augustine] says, “The cross was a pulpit, and the lesson Christ preached on it was love.” O the living love of a dying Savior! #emph[Per vulnera viscera] \[through the wounds, the heart\]. I think I see Christ on the cross, bleeding all over. I think I hear him say to us, “Reach your#super[+] hands here; put them into my side; feel my bleeding heart, and see whether I do not love you#super[+]. And will you#super[+] not give your love to me? Will you#super[+] love the world more than me? Did the world appease the wrath of God for you#super[+]? Have I not done all this? And will you#super[+] not love me?” It is natural to love where we are loved. Christ has set us a copy of love and written it in his blood; let us labor to write after so fair a copy and imitate him in love.

8\. Love to God is the best self-love. It is self-love to get the soul saved, and by loving God we advance our own salvation. 1 John 4:16: “Whoever dwells in love dwells in God, and God in him.” And he who has God dwelling in his heart is sure to dwell with God in heaven. So to love God is the truest self-love; he who does not love God does not love himself.

9\. Love to God is evidence of sincerity (Song 1:4): “The upright love you.” Many a child of God fears he is a hypocrite. Do you love God? When #emph[Peter] was downcast in the sense of his sin, he thought himself unworthy that Christ should ever take notice of him, or employ him any more in the work of his apostleship. See how Christ sets about comforting him (John 21:15): “Simon son of John, do you love Me …?” It is as if Christ had said, “Though you have denied me through fear, yet if you can say from your heart that you love me, you are sincere and upright.” To love God is a better sign of sincerity than to fear him. The #emph[Israelites] feared God’s justice (Ps 78:34): “When He slew them, they would seek Him; they repented and searched for God.” But what did it all come to? (vv. 36, 37): “But they flattered Him with their mouths, and lied to Him with their tongues. Their hearts were disloyal to Him.” That repentance is no better than flattery which arises only from fear of God’s judgments and has no love mixed with it. Loving God is evidence that God has the #emph[heart;] and if the heart is his, it will command all the rest.

10\. By our love to God we may conclude God’s love to us (1 John 4:19): “We love Him because He first loved us.” “Oh,” says the soul, “if I knew God loved me, I could rejoice.” Do you love God? Then you may be sure of God’s love to you. It is as with burning glasses: if the glass burns, it is because the sun has first shone on it; otherwise it could not burn. So if our hearts burn in love to God, it is because God’s love has first shone on us; otherwise we could not burn in love. Our love is nothing but the reflection of God’s love.

11\. If you#super[+] do not love God, you will love something else, either the #emph[world] or #emph[sin.] And are these worthy of your love? Is it not better to love God than these?

First, it is better to love God than the world, as appears in these eight particulars.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. If you#super[+] set your love on worldly things, they will not satisfy. You may as well satisfy your body with air as your soul with earth (Eccl 5:10). Job 20:22: “In the midst of his plenty, he will be distressed.” Plenty has its poverty. If the globe of the world were yours, it would not fill you. #emph[Adam] had two sons, #emph[Cain] and #emph[Abel.] #emph[Cain] means possession; #emph[Abel] means vanity. This shows us that in all our possessions there is vanity. And will you set your love on what will never give you contentment? Is it not better to love God? He will give you what will satisfy (Ps 17:15): “When I awake, I will be satisfied with Your likeness.” When I awake out of the sleep of death, and some of the rays and beams of God’s glory are put on me, then I will be satisfied with his likeness.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. If you#super[+] love worldly things, they cannot remove trouble of mind. If a thorn has got into the conscience, all the world cannot pluck it out. When King #emph[Saul] was troubled in mind, all his crown jewels could not comfort him (1 Sam 28:15). But if you love God, he can give you peace when nothing else can#footnote[χρῆμα γὰρ ἁπάντων τιμαλφέστατον ἡ ἀταραξία. \[For peace of mind is the most precious thing of all.\] #emph[Cyril.]]. He is the one “who turns darkness into dawn” (Amos 5:8). He can drop in Christ’s blood, which is a cooling drink. He can whisper his love by the Spirit and with one smile scatter all your fears and unrest.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. If you#super[+] love the world, you may love what may hinder you from heaven. Worldly comforts may be compared to the wagons of an army: while the soldiers have been getting provisions from the wagons, they have lost the battle. Mark 10:23: “How hard it is for the rich to enter the kingdom of God!” Prosperity is to many like a sail to a boat, which quickly overturns it. So by loving the world you may love what will endanger you#footnote[#emph[In hoc mundo viscosa omnia, & obsessa laqueis.] \[In this world everything is sticky with birdlime and beset with snares.\]]. But if you love God, there is no fear of losing heaven. He will be a rock to hide you, not to hurt you. By loving him we come to enjoy him.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. You#super[+] may love worldly things, and they cannot love you back. You love gold and silver, but your gold cannot love you back. You love a picture, but the picture cannot love you back. You give your love away to the creature and receive no love in return. But if you love God, he will love you back (John 14:23): “If anyone loves Me … My Father will love him, and We will come to him and make Our home with him.” God will not be behind us in love; for our drop we will receive an ocean.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. When you#super[+] love the world, you love what is worse than yourselves. The soul (as #emph[John of Damascus] says) is a spark of heavenly brightness; it carries in it an idea and likeness of God. While you love the world, you love what is infinitely below the worth of your souls. Will anyone spend money on sackcloth? When you lay out your love on the world, you lay out gold on dung; you hang a pearl on a pig; you love what is lower than yourself. Christ speaks of the birds of the air in another sense (Matt 6:26): “Are you#super[+] not much better than they?” So I say of worldly things, “Are you#super[+] not much better than they?” You love a fine house, a beautiful picture; are you not much better than they? But if you love God, you now place your love on the most noble and exalted object; you love what is better than yourselves. God is better than the soul, better than angels, better than heaven.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. You#super[+] may love the world and have hatred in return for your love (John 15:19): “The world hates you#super[+], because you#super[+] are not of the world.” Would it not vex a man to spend money on a piece of ground, and then, instead of bringing forth grain or grapes, it yielded nothing but nettles? So it is with all things under the moon: we love them, and they prove to be nettles that sting. The world is a stepmother: instead of giving the breast, it draws the sword. We meet with nothing but either disappointment or unkindness (Judg 9:15): “May fire come out of the thornbush and consume the cedars of Lebanon.” While we love the creature, fire comes out of this thornbush to devour us. But if we love God, he will not return hatred for love (Prov 8:17): “I love those who love me.” God may chastise, but he cannot hate. Every believer is part of Christ, and God could as soon hate Christ as hate a believer.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[7\. You#super[+] may love the creature too much. You may love wine too much, silver too much, but you cannot love God too much. If it were possible to go too far, excess here would be a virtue. But our sin is that we cannot love God enough (Ezek 16:30): “How weak-willed is your heart.” So it may be said, how weak is our love to God! It is like the water of the last drawing from the still, which has less spirit in it. If we could love God far more than we do, it would still not be in proportion to his worth. So there is no danger of excess in our love to God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[8\. You#super[+] may love worldly things, and they die and leave you. #emph[Riches take wings;] relatives drop away#footnote[οὐδὲν φαινόμενον αἰώνιον. \[Nothing that is seen is eternal.\] #emph[Ignatius.]]. The #emph[Romans] painted the vanity of worldly things in the form of a man: in his right hand a #emph[rose,] in his left hand a #emph[lily,] under his feet #emph[wormwood.] It is an emblem of the world. The rose is sweet and the lily fair, but both are fading; and under the feet is #emph[wormwood:] at death all the delights of the world will be bitter. There is nothing here that lasts#footnote[#emph[Fuit tempus & avidissimos sui deserit, nec quod futurum est, meum est, nec quod fuit; in puncto fugientis temporis pendeo, etc.] \[Time has been, and it deserts even those most greedy of it; what is to come is not mine, nor what has been; I hang on a point of fleeing time.\] Seneca, Letter 72.]. The creature has a little honey in its mouth, but it has wings; it will soon fly away. #emph[Vitae primordium, mortis prodromum] \[the beginning of life is the forerunner of death\]. But if you love God, he is #emph[a portion forever] (Ps 73:26). As he is called a #emph[sun] for comfort, so he is called a #emph[rock] for eternity; he abides forever. So we see it is better to love God than the world.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it is better to love God than sin. What is there in sin that anyone should love it#footnote[ὅπου ἁμαρτία, ἐκεῖ χειμῶν \[where sin is, there is a storm\]]? (1) Sin is a debt: “Forgive us our debts.” It is a debt that binds us over to the wrath of God. Why should we love sin? Does any man love to be in debt? (2) Sin is a disease (Isa 1:5): “The whole head is sick.” And will you love sin? Will any man hug a disease? Will he love his plague-sores? (3) Sin is a pollution. The apostle calls it ῥυπαρία, #emph[filthiness] (Jas 1:21). It is compared to leprosy, to the poison of asps, to vomit. God’s heart rises against sinners (Zech 11:8): “My soul loathed them.” Sin is a misshapen monster. Lust makes a man brutish; malice makes him devilish. What is there in sin to be loved? Shall we love deformity? (4) Sin is an enemy. It is compared to a #emph[serpent] (Prov 23:32). It has four stings: #emph[shame, guilt, horror, death.] Will a man love what seeks his death? Surely, then, it is better to love God than sin#footnote[δι’ ἁμαρτίας αἱ κακώσεις. \[Through sin come miseries.\] #emph[Olympiodorus.]]. God will save you; sin will damn you. Is not he bewitched who loves damnation?]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[12\. The relation we stand in to God calls for love. There is a close bond of marriage (Isa 54:5): “Your husband is your Maker.” And shall not a wife love her husband? He is full of tenderness. His bride is to him as the apple of his eye; he rejoices over her as the bridegroom over the bride (Isa 62:5). He loves the believer as he loves Christ (#emph[John] 17:26), with the same love in #emph[quality,] though not in #emph[equality.] If God is a husband, shall we not love him? The bond calls for affection. Either we must love God, or we give ground to suspect that we are not yet married to him.]

13\. Love is the most lasting grace. It will stay with us when the other graces take their leave. In heaven we will need no repentance, because we will have no sin. In heaven we will not need patience, because there will be no affliction. In heaven there will be no need of faith. Faith looks at things #emph[unseen] (Heb 11:1), but then we will see God #emph[face to face;] and where there is #emph[sight,] there is no need of faith.

But when the other graces are out of date, love continues. In this sense the apostle says #emph[love is greater than faith,] because it lasts longest (1 Cor 13:8): οὐδέποτε ἐκπίπτει, “Love never fails.” Faith is the #emph[Jacob’s] staff we walk with in this life (2 Cor 5:7): “We walk by faith.” But we will set this #emph[Jacob’s] staff down at heaven’s door, and only love will enter. So love carries away the crown from all the other graces. Love is the longest-lived grace; it is a blossom of eternity. How we should strive to excel in this grace, which alone will live with us in heaven and accompany us to the marriage supper of the Lamb!

14\. Love to God will never let sin thrive in the heart. Some plants will not thrive when they are close together, like the laurel and the vine. The love of God withers sin. Though the #emph[old man] lives, he is like a #emph[sick man:] he is weak, and his breath is short. Love, like the #emph[water of jealousy,] makes the thigh of sin rot. The flower of love kills the weed of sin. Though sin does not die completely, it dies daily. How we should labor for that grace, which is the only #emph[aqua fortis,] the strong acid, to destroy sin!

15\. Love to God is an excellent means for growth in grace (2 Pet 3:18): “But grow in the grace.” Growth in grace is very pleasing to God. Christ #emph[accepts] the truth of grace, but #emph[commends] the degrees of grace. And what can promote and increase grace more than love to God? Love is like watering the root, which makes the tree grow. That is why the apostle uses this expression in his prayer (2 Thess 3:5): “May the Lord direct your hearts into God’s love.” He knew that this grace of love would nurse and cherish all the graces.

16\. Consider the great benefit that will come to us if we love God (1 Cor 2:9): “No eye has seen, no ear has heard, no heart has imagined, what God has prepared for those who love Him.” The eye has seen rare sights; the ear has heard sweet music. “But no eye has seen, no ear has heard, no heart has imagined, what God has prepared for those who love Him!” Such glorious rewards are laid up that (as #emph[Augustine] says) faith itself is not able to grasp them. God has promised a crown of life to those who love him (Jas 1:12). This crown encircles within it all blessedness, riches, glory, and delight, and it is a crown that never fades away (1 Pet 5:4). In this way God would win us to love him by rewards.

17\. Love to God is armor of proof against error. For lack of hearts full of love, men have heads full of error. Unholy opinions come from the lack of holy affections. Why are men given up to “a powerful delusion,” except that “they refused the love of the truth” (2 Thess 2:10, 11)? The more we love God, the more we hate those unorthodox opinions that would draw us away from God into libertinism. The more a man loves health, the more he hates #emph[mercury.]

18\. If we love God, we have all winds blowing for us; everything in the world will conspire for our good. We do not know what #emph[fiery trials] we may meet with, but for those who love God #emph[all things will work for good.] The things that work #emph[against] them will work #emph[for them.] Their cross will make way for a crown; every wind will blow them to the heavenly port.

19\. Lack of love to God is the root of apostasy. The seed in the parable that had no root fell away. He who does not have the love of God rooted in his heart will fall away in time of temptation. He who loves God will cling to him as #emph[Ruth] clung to #emph[Naomi] (Ruth 1:16): “Wherever you go, I will go … Where you die, I will die.” But he who lacks love to God will do as #emph[Orpah] did to her mother-in-law: she kissed her and said goodbye to her. A soldier who has no love for his commander will leave him when he sees an opportunity and run over to the enemy’s side. He who has no love to God in his heart, you may mark down as an apostate.

20\. Love is the only thing in which we can repay God in kind. If God is angry with us, we must not be angry in return. If he rebukes us, we must not rebuke him in return. But if God loves us, we must love him in return. There is nothing in which we can answer God in kind but love. We must not give him word for word, but we must give him love for love. So we have seen twenty motives to stir up and kindle our love to God.

=== The Means of Love

Question. #emph[How shall we come to love God?]

#emph[Answer.] 1. Study God. If we studied him more, we would love him more. Take a view of his surpassing excellencies#footnote[εἰ δύνασαι νοῆσαι τὸν θεὸν, νοήσεις τὸ ἀγαθὸν ὑπέρλαμπρον, καὶ ὑπερλαμπόμενον. \[If you can conceive of God, you will conceive of the good, surpassing in brightness and outshining all.\] #emph[Hermes Trismegistus.]]: his holiness, his incomprehensible goodness. The angels know God better than we do and clearly see the splendor of his majesty, and that is why they are so deeply in love with him.

2\. Labor to have an interest in God, to be able to call him yours (Ps 63:1): “O God, You are my God.” That pronoun #emph[my] is #emph[suavissima amoris illecebra,] a sweet magnet to love; a man loves what is his own. The more we believe, the more we love. Faith is the root, and love is the flower that grows on it (Gal 5:6): “faith, working through love.”

3\. Make it your earnest request to God that he will give you a heart to love him.#footnote[#emph[Dilectio Dei, donum Dei.] \[The love of God is the gift of God.\] Augustine.] This is an acceptable request; surely God will not deny it. King #emph[Solomon] asked God for wisdom (1 Kgs 3:9): “Give Your servant an understanding heart.” Verse 10: “It pleased the Lord.” So when you cry to God, “Lord, give me a heart to love you; it is my grief that I can love you no more; O kindle this fire from heaven on the altar of my heart,” surely this prayer pleases the Lord, and he will pour out his Spirit on you, whose golden oil will make the lamp of your love burn bright.

=== Preserving Love from a Consumption

2\. I proceed to the second branch of the exhortation. You#super[+] who have love to God, labor to preserve it; do not let this love die and be quenched#footnote[2 #emph[Branch]]. As you would have God’s love continued to you, let your love be continued to him. Love, like fire, will be ready to go out (Rev 2:4): “You have abandoned your first love.” Satan labors to blow out this flame, and through neglect of duty we lose it. When a delicate body leaves off its clothes, it is apt to catch cold. So when we leave off duty, we cool by degrees in our love to God. Of all the graces, love is the quickest to decay; so we need to be all the more careful to preserve it. If a man has a jewel, he will keep it; if he has land by inheritance, he will keep it. How careful, then, should we be to keep this grace of love? It is sad to see professing Christians declining in their love to God. Many are in a #emph[spiritual consumption;] their love is wasting away.

There are four signs by which Christians may know that their love is in a consumption.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. When they have lost their taste. He who is far gone in a consumption has no taste; he does not find the same savory relish in his food as before. So when Christians have lost their taste and find no sweetness in a promise, it is a sign of a spiritual consumption (1 Pet 2:3): “now that you#super[+] have tasted that the Lord is good.” There was a time when they found comfort in drawing near to God. His word was like dripping honey, very delicious to the palate of their soul. But now it is otherwise: they can taste no more sweetness in spiritual things than in “the white of an egg” (Job 6:6). This is a sign that they are in a consumption. To lose the taste shows the loss of the first love.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. When Christians have lost their appetite. A man far gone in a consumption does not have the same stomach for his food as before. There was a time when Christians did #emph[hunger and thirst for righteousness.] They cared for things of a heavenly kind: the grace of the Spirit, the blood of the cross, the light of God’s face. They had a stomach for ordinances and came to them like a hungry man to a feast. But now the case is altered. They have no appetite; they do not prize Christ as they did; they do not have such strong affections for the word; their hearts #emph[are not burning within them.] It is a sad sign that they are in a consumption and their love is wasting away. It was a sign that #emph[David’s] natural strength had failed when they covered him with clothes and yet he got no heat (1 Kgs 1:1). So when men are plied with hot clothes, I mean #emph[ordinances,] yet have no heat of affection, but are cold and stiff, as if they were ready to be laid out for burial, it is a sign that their first love has declined. They are far gone in a consumption.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. When Christians grow more in love with the world, it shows a decrease of spiritual love. They were once of an exalted, heavenly temper; they spoke the #emph[language of Canaan.] But now they are like the fish in the gospel that had #emph[money in its mouth] (Matt 17:27). They cannot utter three words without one of them being about money. Their thoughts and affections (like Satan) are always #emph[roaming through the earth.] It is a sign that they are going quickly downhill; their love to God is in a consumption. We may observe that when nature decays and grows weaker, people walk more stooped. And truly, when the heart stoops more to the earth, and is so bent over that it can hardly lift itself up to a heavenly thought, it is now sadly declining in its first love. When rust clings to metal, it not only takes away the metal’s brightness, but eats into it and consumes it. So when the earth clings to men’s souls, it not only dims the shining luster of their graces, but by degrees eats away their graces.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. When Christians make little account of the worship of God. They perform the duties of religion in a dead, formal manner. If the duties are not left #emph[undone,] they are #emph[badly done.] This is a sad symptom of a spiritual consumption; slackness in duty shows a decay in our first love. When the strings of a viol are slack, the viol can never make good music. When men grow slack in duty, they pray as if they did not pray, and this can never make any harmonious sound in God’s ears. When the spiritual motion, like that of the eighth sphere, is slow and heavy, and the pulse of the soul beats low, it is a sign that Christians have #emph[abandoned their first love.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Let us beware of this spiritual consumption. It is dangerous to slacken in our love. Love is a grace we do not know how to do without. A soldier may as well be without his weapons, a painter without his brush, a musician without his viol, as a Christian be without love. The body cannot do without its natural heat. Love is to the soul what natural heat is to the body; there is no living without it. Love moves the graces, stirs up the affections, makes us grieve for sin, and makes us cheerful in God. It is like oil to the wheels; it quickens us in God’s service. How careful, then, we should be to keep divine love alive!]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Question. #emph[How may we keep our love from going out?]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Answer.] Watch your#super[+] hearts every day. Take notice of the first declines in grace. Observe yourselves when you begin to grow dull and listless, and use every means of quickening. Be much in prayer, meditation, and holy conversation. When the fire is going out, you throw on fuel. So when the flame of your love is going out, make use of ordinances and gospel promises as fuel to keep the fire of your love burning.]]

=== Increasing Love to God

3\. Let me exhort Christians to increase in love to God. Let your love be boiled up higher (Phil 1:9)#footnote[3 #emph[Branch.]]: “And this is my prayer: that your love may abound μᾶλλον καὶ μᾶλλον, more and more.” Our love to God should be like the light of the morning. First there is the #emph[crepusculum,] or daybreak; then it shines brighter until full noon. Those who have a few sparks of love should ἀναζωπυρεῖν, blow up those divine sparks into a flame. A Christian should not be content with so small a dram of grace that it makes him doubt whether he has any grace or not. He should keep on increasing the stock. He who has a little gold would like more; you who love God a little, labor to love him more. A godly man is like a man with dropsy: the more grace he drinks in, the more he thirsts. He is content with very little of the world, yet he is never satisfied, but would have more of the Spirit’s influence, and labors to add one degree of love to another. To persuade Christians to put more oil in the lamp and increase the flame of their love, let me set before them these four divine incentives.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The growth of love is evidence of its truth. If I see the almond tree bud and flourish, I know there is life in the root. Paint will not grow; a hypocrite, who is only a picture, will not grow. But where we see love to God increasing and growing bigger (like #emph[Elijah’s] cloud), we may conclude that it is true and genuine.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. By the growth of love we imitate the saints in the Bible. Their love to God, like the #emph[waters of the sanctuary,] rose higher. The disciples’ love to Christ was weak at first: #emph[they fled from Christ.] But after Christ’s death it grew more vigorous, and they openly professed him. #emph[Peter’s] love was weaker and more feeble at first; he denied Christ. But afterward, how boldly he preached him (Acts 4:10)! Indeed, when Christ put his love to the test (John 21:16), “Simon son of John, do you love Me?”, #emph[Peter] could make his humble yet confident appeal to Christ: “Yes, Lord … You know I love You.” So that tender plant, which before was blown down by the wind of a temptation, has now grown into a cedar that all the powers of hell could not shake.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The growth of love will enlarge the reward. The more we burn in love, the more we will shine in glory. The higher our love, the heavier our crown.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. The more we love God, the more love we will have from him. Would we have God open to us the sweet secrets of his love? Would we have the smiles of his face, the kisses of his lips? Oh, then let us strive for higher degrees of love. #emph[Paul] counted gold and pearls as dung compared with Christ (Phil 3:8). Indeed, he was so on fire with love to God that he could have wished himself #emph[cursed and cut off from Christ for the sake of his brothers the Jews] (Rom 9:3). Not that he could be cut off from Christ; but such was his fervent love and godly zeal for the glory of God that he would have been content to suffer, even beyond what is fit to speak of, if God might have more honor.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Here was love screwed up to the highest pitch that a mortal could possibly reach. And see how near he lay to God’s heart! The Lord makes known to him the #emph[arcana coeli] \[the secrets of heaven\]. He takes him up to heaven for a while and lays him in his bosom, where he had such a glorious sight of God and heard those ἄρρητα ῥήματα, “things too sacred for words, things that man is not permitted to tell” (2 Cor 12:4). No man was ever a loser by his love to God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. If our love to God does not increase, it will soon decrease. If the fire is not blown up, it will quickly go out. So Christians should above all things try to cherish and stir up their love to God. This exhortation will be out of date when we come to heaven, for then our light will be clear and our love perfect. But now it is in season to exhort that our love to God may abound #emph[more and more.]]]
